    "probe": "hb-shape widths per class of two production Arabic bodies, cloud cut over the stand-in; Word PDF of 8 single lines = hb within 0.1%; box = hhea 2416/979 over 2048"
  },
  "note": "Simplified Arabic (M365 cloud face Word renders real) is a compact naskh. Class scales are harfbuzz shaped widths of the cloud cut over the stand-in, measured over two production Arabic bodies (57k letters, 2026-11-08) and checked against a Word for Mac PDF of 8 single-line sentences (Word = harfbuzz within 0.1%): Arabic letters 0.858 of the Noto Naskh subset (bold cut 0.880; weight-normal only so Blink synthesizes bold and bold runs keep the regular ratio), Arabic-Indic digits, Arabic punctuation (incl. the U+066A-066D separators) and the ornate brackets each on their own ratio; tatweel, ZWNJ and ZWJ stay in the letter face because Blink shapes each unicode-range face as its own run and a split joiner would break the cursive connection (the cloud tatweel is far narrower, 0.26 / 0.34, an accepted drift), and vs Liberation Sans/Helvetica: ASCII digits, Latin letters (mixed-case English prose, 0.922; the Noto subset has no Latin so the letters would otherwise leave the alias), space, dots, brackets, other punctuation, guillemets, dashes. The earlier single 0.800 / 0.924 ratio (2026-09-11 pangram) ran 6-13% wide on real text. Every face pins the Word hhea box.",
